<!-- ===== HEADER ===== -->
<header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Biomedical Research Landscape 2024–2026</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">Publication Trends, Topic Dynamics, and Open Access Patterns — a trend analysis of 126,832 biomedical research abstracts indexed in PubMed</div>
    <div class="meta">Data Source: NCBI PubMed (Jan 2024 – Mar 2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Analysis sample: 4,886 records &nbsp;|&nbsp; Report date: April 2026</div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ===== KPI STRIP ===== -->
<div class="container">
  <div class="kpi-strip">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">4,886</div>
      <div class="label">Total Publications</div>
      <div class="detail">2024: 3,924 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2025: 737 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2026: 225</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value" style="color:#2f855a">65.1%</div>
      <div class="label">Open Access Rate</div>
      <div class="detail">3,183 of 4,886 articles available via PMC</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value" style="color:#805ad5">6</div>
      <div class="label">Median Authors</div>
      <div class="detail">Mean: 7.1 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Stable across all three years</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value" style="color:#c05621">51.7%</div>
      <div class="label">Top 10 Countries Share</div>
      <div class="detail">China (21.0%) and USA (10.7%) lead</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value" style="color:#b83280">+380%</div>
      <div class="label">Top Emerging Topic Growth</div>
      <div class="detail">Coleoptera: 0.30% → 1.44% share (2024→2025)</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===== SECTION 1: PUBLICATION VOLUME ===== -->
  <div class="section" id="publication-volume-trends">
    <h2>1. Publication Volume &amp; Temporal Distribution</h2>

    <p>The dataset contains <strong>4,886 biomedical abstracts</strong> from the full population of 126,832 PubMed-indexed records spanning January 2024 through March 2026. As expected from PubMed indexing dynamics, <strong>80.3% of records are from 2024</strong>, with 2025 contributing 15.1% and early 2026 just 4.6%. This temporal skew reflects real-world indexing lag — older publications accumulate more indexed entries over time — and must be accounted for in any cross-year comparison.</p>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <strong>Indexing Lag Advisory:</strong> The drop in volume from 2024 to 2025–2026 is primarily an artifact of PubMed's indexing pipeline, not a decline in research output. 2025 and 2026 data should be interpreted as incomplete snapshots; normalized rates and proportions are more reliable than absolute counts for cross-year comparisons.
    </div>

    <div class="chart-row">
      <div class="chart-box full">
        <h4>Monthly Publication Volume (Known Months Only)</h4>
        <canvas id="chartMonthly"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Yearly Breakdown</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Year</th><th>Publications</th><th>Share</th><th>Known Month</th><th>Unknown Month</th></tr>
      <tr><td>2024</td><td>3,924</td><td>80.3%</td><td>3,349</td><td>575 (14.7%)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2025</td><td>737</td><td>15.1%</td><td>558</td><td>179 (24.3%)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2026</td><td>225</td><td>4.6%</td><td>225</td><td>0</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p style="margin-top: 14px;">January 2024 shows a pronounced spike (1,665 publications), reflecting the accumulation of early-year indexed content. From February onward, 2024 monthly volumes stabilize between 35–390, with a gentle downward slope through the year as newer records await indexing. The 2025 monthly volumes are more uniform (26–94 per month), and early 2026 shows an encouraging uptick: January (51), February (76), March (98) — suggesting accelerating indexing of recent output.</p>

    <p>Notably, <strong>15.4% of all records (754)</strong> have unknown publication months, concentrated in 2024 (575) and 2025 (179). These are excluded from monthly trend charts but included in yearly aggregates.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ===== SECTION 2: GEOGRAPHIC LANDSCAPE ===== -->
  <div class="section" id="geographic-landscape">
    <h2>2. Geographic Landscape of Biomedical Research</h2>

    <p>Research output is dominated by a handful of prolific nations. <strong>China leads decisively with 21.0% of all publications (1,028)</strong>, more than double the USA's contribution (521 articles, 10.7%). India (196, 4.0%), Japan (137, 2.8%), and Iran/Canada (107 each, 2.2%) round out the top contributors. Together, the top 10 countries account for <strong>51.7% of all publications</strong>.</p>

    <div class="chart-row">
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Top 10 Countries by Publication Volume</h4>
        <canvas id="chartCountries"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>China &amp; USA Share Over Time</h4>
        <canvas id="chartCountryTrend"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Geographic Shifts 2024 → 2025</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Country</th><th>2024 Share</th><th>2025 Share</th><th>Trend</th></tr>
      <tr><td>China</td><td>22.6%</td><td>13.2%</td><td>Declining share</td></tr>
      <tr><td>USA</td><td>10.9%</td><td>9.6%</td><td>Slightly declining</td></tr>
      <tr><td>India</td><td>3.3%</td><td>8.0%</td><td><strong>Strong growth (+143%)</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Iran</td><td>2.0%</td><td>3.7%</td><td>Growing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Japan</td><td>2.9%</td><td>2.4%</td><td>Stable</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout success">
      <strong>Key Finding:</strong> India's share of indexed publications more than doubled from 3.3% in 2024 to 8.0% in 2025, making it the third-largest contributor. Iran also shows notable growth (2.0% → 3.7%). These shifts may reflect both genuine output increases and differential indexing timelines.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===== SECTION 3: TOPICS & MESH ===== -->
  <div class="section" id="topic-and-mesh-analysis">
    <h2>3. Research Topic &amp; MeSH Keyword Analysis</h2>

    <p>Of the 4,886 records, <strong>2,583 (52.9%) have assigned MeSH major topics</strong>. The remaining 47.1% lack MeSH indexing, a common pattern for newer publications still in PubMed's curation pipeline. Analysis below is restricted to MeSH-indexed records.</p>

    <div class="chart-row">
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Top 15 Major Topics (All Years)</h4>
        <canvas id="chartTopics"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Emerging Topics: Share Growth 2024→2025</h4>
        <canvas id="chartEmerging"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Dominant Research Topics</h3>
    <p><strong>COVID-19 remains the single most prevalent major topic</strong> (43 articles, 1.7% of MeSH-indexed records), though its dominance is waning: 37 publications in 2024 but only 5 in 2025. Type 2 Diabetes (24), Breast Neoplasms (17), Gastrointestinal Microbiome (16), and Alzheimer Disease (15) form the next tier of high-activity areas.</p>

    <h3>Emerging &amp; Accelerating Topics</h3>
    <p>Proportional share growth from 2024 to 2025 reveals several fast-accelerating topics:</p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Topic</th><th>2024 Count (Share)</th><th>2025 Count (Share)</th><th>Share Growth</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Air Pollution</td><td>1 (0.04%)</td><td>2 (0.96%)</td><td>+2,139%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Appendicitis</td><td>2 (0.09%)</td><td>2 (0.96%)</td><td>+1,020%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PTSD</td><td>3 (0.13%)</td><td>2 (0.96%)</td><td>+646%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>COPD</td><td>3 (0.13%)</td><td>2 (0.96%)</td><td>+646%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Electroencephalography</td><td>4 (0.17%)</td><td>2 (0.96%)</td><td>+460%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Coleoptera</td><td>7 (0.30%)</td><td>3 (1.44%)</td><td>+380%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Machine Learning</td><td>3 (0.13%)</td><td>1 (0.48%)</td><td>+273%</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <strong>Small-Sample Caution:</strong> Growth rates are computed from proportional shares across MeSH-indexed records. With 2025 containing only 208 MeSH-indexed articles, absolute counts are small and individual percentage changes should be interpreted as directional signals, not robust trends.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===== SECTION 4: OPEN ACCESS ===== -->
  <div class="section" id="open-access-patterns">
    <h2>4. Open Access Adoption &amp; Journal Landscape</h2>

    <p>Overall, <strong>65.1% of all publications (3,183 of 4,886) are open access</strong>, available through PubMed Central. The OA rate varies meaningfully by year: <strong>64.1% in 2024, 74.1% in 2025, and 53.8% in early 2026</strong>. The 2025 peak likely reflects the composition of recently indexed articles (newer articles in PMC-participating journals are indexed faster), while the 2026 dip may be a timing artifact as PMC ingestion catches up.</p>

    <div class="chart-row">
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Open Access Rate by Year</h4>
        <canvas id="chartOAYear"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-box">
        <h4>Open Access Rate by Research Type</h4>
        <canvas id="chartOAType"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-row">
      <div class="chart-box full">
        <h4>Monthly Open Access Rate (Known Months Only)</h4>
        <canvas id="chartOAMonthly"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>OA by Research Type</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Research Type</th><th>Count</th><th>OA Rate</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Preprint</td><td>41</td><td><strong>100.0%</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Case Report</td><td>191</td><td>83.2%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clinical Trial</td><td>30</td><td>70.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Review</td><td>420</td><td>64.8%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Journal Article</td><td>4,119</td><td>64.1%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Systematic Review</td><td>85</td><td>56.5%</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h3>OA by Country (Top 10)</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Country</th><th>Publications</th><th>OA Rate</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Iran</td><td>107</td><td><strong>86.9%</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>100</td><td>78.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Japan</td><td>137</td><td>77.4%</td></tr>
