      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>China dominates output but trails on open access.</strong> With 21% of all publications, China is the leading producer by a wide margin. However, its 55.7% OA rate is the lowest among top-10 countries. <em>Implication: Mandates or incentives for OA publishing in Chinese institutions could meaningfully shift global access to biomedical knowledge.</em>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <div class="takeaway-num">2</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>Open access is the majority norm — and trending upward.</strong> At 65.1% overall and 74.1% in 2025, OA publishing has become the default for most biomedical research. Preprints are 100% OA by definition, and case reports achieve 83.2%. <em>Implication: Funders can increasingly rely on OA availability; remaining closed-access pockets are concentrated in certain countries and systematic reviews (56.5% OA).</em>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <div class="takeaway-num">3</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>India is an emerging research powerhouse.</strong> India's share of publications more than doubled from 3.3% (2024) to 8.0% (2025), surging past Japan, Canada, and the UK. <em>Implication: Research partnerships and funding collaborations with Indian institutions should be a strategic priority for international organizations.</em>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <div class="takeaway-num">4</div>
      <div class="takeaway-text">
        <strong>COVID-19 is fading as the dominant research topic.</strong> While still the most-indexed MeSH term overall (43 articles), COVID-19 publications dropped from 37 in 2024 to just 5 in 2025. The field is diversifying toward chronic conditions: diabetes, oncology, neurodegeneration, and microbiome research. <em>Implication: Funding strategies should pivot toward the rising tide of non-communicable disease research.</em>
      </div>
    </div>

